        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**Politics** is collective, about deciding, and the decision becomes binding **policy** for the whole group. Two conceptions: serving the community (Aristotle) against a competitive struggle for power (Lasswell: \"who gets what, when, how\"). **Comparison** is \"the methodological core of the scientific study of politics\" (Powell et al.), with six benefits: description, context, rules, understanding, prediction, making choices.\n\n**Government** is the institutions and offices that make binding decisions; **governance** is the process and quality of collective decision-making, in which government is one actor among many. **Power** is the capacity to act, **authority** the acknowledged right to do so; the lecture adds **legitimacy**: the governed recognise and accept the right to decide. **Lukes**: who prevails (decision-making), who controls the agenda (non-decision-making), who shapes preferences (ideological). **Weber**: tradition, charisma, legal-rational norms.\n\n**Typologies**: no generally accepted one exists. The book uses the EIU **Democracy Index** (out of 10: full democracy, flawed democracy, hybrid regime, authoritarian regime) and **Freedom House** (out of 100: free, partly free, not free).\n\n**From the lecture:** a policy is a decision, a **non-decision**, or an intention to make future decisions; the state is territory, population and government; three branches (executive, legislative, judiciary); **public governance** and **metagovernance** (the power of governing governance), which you find by reading the constitution."
