        "type": "tekst",
        "tekst": "**What makes a government legitimate?**\n\n**Formally**, democratic legitimacy means that government policies and actions, **including appointments**, rest on laws made by elected representatives or by direct vote (a referendum), within the limits of the constitution, which also protects minority rights. The slide's three foundations: **rule of law, consent, participation**.\n\nBut that is **necessary, not sufficient**. A government that follows every rule and still gets nothing done loses legitimacy too. **Dwight Waldo** (1948): \"without results we know that democracy means nothing and ceases to be alive in the minds and hearts of men\". So legitimacy rests on two legs: making policy the right way **and** carrying it out effectively.\n\nThe lecture also lists **eight qualities of good governance** (in liberal democracies): participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus-oriented, equity and inclusiveness, effectiveness and efficiency, accountability. They are not in the chapter."
