        "type": "tekst",
        "tekst": "**Waldo's paradox**\n\nGetting results requires **bureaucracy**, which Weber saw as unmatched at getting things done. That creates a paradox: we believe in democracy, and at the same time we trust bureaucratic administration to be superior.\n\nThe authors' resolution: they are good at **different values**. **Democratic values**: responsiveness, representativeness, participation, transparency. **Bureaucratic values**: efficiency, expertise, control, order. **Effectiveness and accountability** are served from both sides (Figure 2.1).\n\nTwo asymmetries: bureaucracy is a **means** that can serve any regime, democratic or authoritarian; and bureaucracy is largely **self-legitimating** (Weber), while democracy needs **constant attention** to stay legitimate."
