        "tekst": "Participation runs from **conventional** to **unconventional** to **illegal** (Table 13.1). **Gladiators, spectators, apathetics** (Milbrath and Goel; slide: about 5-7, 60 and 35 per cent). High-status groups participate more because of **resources and interest** (Verba et al.). The **paradox of participation** (Downs, 1957), and five reasons people engage anyway: idealism, responsibility, being heard, mobilisation, enjoyment. Four influences on voter choice: **class, the economy, issues, religion**. Public opinion as a **prompt** or a **veto**. In authoritarian regimes: patronage, clientelism (the slide: an insurance policy), mobilised participation.\n\n**Parties**: five roles (government, guidance, aggregation, mobilisation, recruitment; the lecture calls recruitment the main one); **partisan dealignment**; cadre, mass, catch-all, niche and cartel parties; five **party systems** (no-party, single-party, dominant party, two-party, multi-party); Michels's **iron law of oligarchy**, \"actually not so iron at all\"; candidate selection from open primary to party leader; public funding now all but universal in democracies."
