        "titel": "Session 6: integrity and global governance (Huberts et al.; Maia)",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**Integrity**: of four mainstream views, the PI-Index uses acting in accordance with the relevant moral and legal values and norms. **Ten types of integrity violations** (Table 1), with corruption as \"the abuse of entrusted power for private gain\" (Transparency International). A **scandal** needs a transgression that becomes known, public denunciation and possible damage to reputation (Thompson). **PI-Index 2013-2019**: 355 scandals, 282 local (79%), misconduct in the private sphere the most common type (106, 30%).\n\n**Global governance**: \"the collective effort to identify, understand, and address worldwide problems that are beyond the capacity of individual states\"; \"something that happens, no one apparently, actually does it\". **Five bases of authority**: institutional, delegated, expert, principled, capacity-based. The **Security Council** has institutional and delegated authority, no expert authority. The veto: \"performance, unity, and control, not equity\". After Resolution 1973 on Libya (2011), Russian vetoes on Syria meant a **freezing** of global governance."
